Labeled formal units asserted in the argument — theorems, proofs, definitions, and the definitional structures.

<section #eh-corollary | `<corollary>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<corollary>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the corollary's label, parallel to &lt;theorem&gt;'s `name` kwarg. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this corollary participates in the propositional theorem-family shared counter. |

Produced by: canonical `<corollary>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-corollary-1>
<corollary | Every prime greater than 2 is odd.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-corollary-1>
<corollary | Every prime greater than 2 is odd.>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:eh-corollary-2>
<corollary #cor:bounded>
Any continuous function on a closed interval is bounded.
</corollary>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-corollary-2>
<corollary #cor:bounded>
Any continuous function on a closed interval is bounded.
</corollary>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #eh-dd | `<dd>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · eHTML `<dd>` · JATS `<def>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<dd>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-dd-1>
<dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-dd-1>
<dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</minipage>

*A definition-list description. Appears as a child of &lt;dl&gt;,
following the term it defines.*

<code-block #code:eh-dd-2>
<dd |
A multi-paragraph definition.

The second paragraph of the definition.
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-dd-2>
<dd |
A multi-paragraph definition.

The second paragraph of the definition.
>
</minipage>

*Multi-paragraph descriptions are valid — the pipe content's
paragraph structure is preserved.*

</section>

<section #eh-definition | `<definition>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<definition>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the definition's label. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this definition participates in the definition counter. |

Produced by: canonical `<definition>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-definition-1>
<definition | A *group* is a set with an associative binary operation, identity, and inverses.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-definition-1>
<definition | A *group* is a set with an associative binary operation, identity, and inverses.>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:eh-definition-2>
<definition name="Group" #def:group>
A *group* is a set $G$ together with a binary operation
$\cdot$ satisfying associativity, identity, and inverses.
</definition>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-definition-2>
<definition name="Group" #def:group>
A *group* is a set $G$ together with a binary operation
$\cdot$ satisfying associativity, identity, and inverses.
</definition>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #eh-dl | `<dl>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · eHTML `<dl>` · JATS `<def-list>` · `content` `empty` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<dl>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:eh-dl-1>
<dl>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
  <dt | eHTML>
  <dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
  <dt | Enscribe shorthand>
  <dd | The shorthand authoring syntax that compiles to eHTML.>
</dl>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-dl-1>
<dl>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
  <dt | eHTML>
  <dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
  <dt | Enscribe shorthand>
  <dd | The shorthand authoring syntax that compiles to eHTML.>
</dl>
</minipage>

*Long-form &lt;dl&gt; with short-form &lt;dt&gt;/&lt;dd&gt; children. The natural
authoring pattern.*

<code-block #code:eh-dl-2>
<dl .compact>
  <dt | term-1>
  <dd | First definition of term-1.>
  <dd | Second definition of term-1.>
</dl>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-dl-2>
<dl .compact>
  <dt | term-1>
  <dd | First definition of term-1.>
  <dd | Second definition of term-1.>
</dl>
</minipage>

*One term with multiple definitions — a valid HTML pattern.*

</section>

<section #eh-dt | `<dt>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · eHTML `<dt>` · JATS `<term>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<dt>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-dt-1>
<dt | enscribe>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-dt-1>
<dt | enscribe>
</minipage>

*A definition-list term. Appears as a child of &lt;dl&gt;.*

<code #code:eh-dt-2>
<dt | <code | strict-mode>>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-dt-2>
<dt | <code | strict-mode>>
</minipage>

*Inline markup in a term. The recursive-content pass parses the
pipe content normally.*

</section>

<section #eh-example | `<example>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<example>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the example's label. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this example participates in the example counter. |

Produced by: canonical `<example>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-example-1>
<example | The integers under addition form a group.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-example-1>
<example | The integers under addition form a group.>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:eh-example-2>
<example #ex:integers>
The integers $\mathbb{Z}$ under addition form a group: the
operation is associative, $0$ is the identity, and every
integer has an additive inverse.
</example>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-example-2>
<example #ex:integers>
The integers $\mathbb{Z}$ under addition form a group: the
operation is associative, $0$ is the identity, and every
integer has an additive inverse.
</example>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #eh-glossary | `<glossary>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · eHTML `<glossary>` · JATS `<glossary>` · `content` `empty` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<glossary>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:eh-glossary-1>
<glossary #project-terms>
  <glossary-entry>
    <dt | enscribe>
    <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
  </glossary-entry>
  <glossary-entry>
    <dt | eHTML>
    <dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
  </glossary-entry>
</glossary>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-glossary-1>
<glossary #project-terms>
  <glossary-entry>
    <dt | enscribe>
    <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
  </glossary-entry>
  <glossary-entry>
    <dt | eHTML>
    <dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
  </glossary-entry>
</glossary>
</minipage>

*A glossary with two entries. Each &lt;glossary-entry&gt; uses &lt;dt&gt;/&lt;dd&gt;
for its term and definition (the same shapes &lt;dl&gt; uses), wrapped
in the entry's own envelope for cross-reference / styling.*

</section>

<section #eh-glossary-entry | `<glossary-entry>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · eHTML `<glossary-entry>` · JATS `<def-item>` · `content` `empty` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<glossary-entry>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:eh-glossary-entry-1>
<glossary-entry #term:enscribe>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</glossary-entry>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-glossary-entry-1>
<glossary-entry #term:enscribe>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</glossary-entry>
</minipage>

*A single glossary entry. The id uses the "term:" colon-prefix
convention so cross-references like &lt;ref @term:enscribe&gt; can
resolve into the entry.*

</section>

<section #eh-lemma | `<lemma>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<lemma>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the lemma's label, parallel to &lt;theorem&gt;'s `name` kwarg. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this lemma participates in the propositional theorem- family shared counter. |

Produced by: canonical `<lemma>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-lemma-1>
<lemma | Every continuous function on a compact set attains its maximum.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-lemma-1>
<lemma | Every continuous function on a compact set attains its maximum.>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:eh-lemma-2>
<lemma name="Zorn" #lem:zorn>
Every non-empty partially-ordered set in which every chain has
an upper bound contains a maximal element.
</lemma>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-lemma-2>
<lemma name="Zorn" #lem:zorn>
Every non-empty partially-ordered set in which every chain has
an upper bound contains a maximal element.
</lemma>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #eh-proof | `<proof>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<proof>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix — e.g. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default off | Whether this proof is numbered. |

Produced by: canonical `<proof>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:eh-proof-1>
<theorem | The sum of two even integers is even.>

<proof>
Let $a = 2m$ and $b = 2n$. Then $a + b = 2(m + n)$, which is
even. $\square$
</proof>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-proof-1>
<theorem | The sum of two even integers is even.>

<proof>
Let $a = 2m$ and $b = 2n$. Then $a + b = 2(m + n)$, which is
even. $\square$
</proof>
</minipage>

*The canonical pattern: &lt;theorem&gt; followed by sibling &lt;proof&gt;.
The proof is NOT nested inside the theorem.*

<code-block #code:eh-proof-2>
<proof name="of Theorem 1.2">
The argument follows from the preceding lemma.
</proof>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-proof-2>
<proof name="of Theorem 1.2">
The argument follows from the preceding lemma.
</proof>
</minipage>

*Optional `name` kwarg lets the proof identify what it proves
(useful when the proof is separated from its theorem by
intervening text).*

</section>

<section #eh-proposition | `<proposition>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<proposition>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the proposition's label. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this proposition participates in the propositional theorem-family shared counter. |

Produced by: canonical `<proposition>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-proposition-1>
<proposition | The sum of two even integers is even.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-proposition-1>
<proposition | The sum of two even integers is even.>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:eh-proposition-2>
<proposition name="Cauchy-Schwarz">
For any vectors $u$, $v$ in an inner-product space,
$|\langle u, v \rangle| \le \|u\| \, \|v\|$.
</proposition>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-proposition-2>
<proposition name="Cauchy-Schwarz">
For any vectors $u$, $v$ in an inner-product space,
$|\langle u, v \rangle| \le \|u\| \, \|v\|$.
</proposition>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #eh-remark | `<remark>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<remark>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the remark's label. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default off | Whether this remark is numbered. |

Produced by: canonical `<remark>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-remark-1>
<remark | The converse does not hold in general.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-remark-1>
<remark | The converse does not hold in general.>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:eh-remark-2>
<remark>
The hypothesis of compactness is essential here; without it
the conclusion fails (consider $f(x) = 1/x$ on $(0, 1]$).
</remark>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-remark-2>
<remark>
The hypothesis of compactness is essential here; without it
the conclusion fails (consider $f(x) = 1/x$ on $(0, 1]$).
</remark>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #eh-theorem | `<theorem>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · eHTML `<theorem>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the theorem's label (the "(Pythagoras)" half of "Theorem 1.2 (Pythagoras)"). |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this theorem participates in the theorem-family shared counter (see "Numbering" below). |

Produced by: canonical `<theorem>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-theorem-1>
<theorem | If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-theorem-1>
<theorem | If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.>
</minipage>

*Short-form with pipe content. The body is parsed normally.*

<code-block #code:eh-theorem-2>
<theorem name="Pythagoras" #thm:pyth>
If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, the triangle with sides $a$, $b$, $c$
is right-angled.
</theorem>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-theorem-2>
<theorem name="Pythagoras" #thm:pyth>
If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, the triangle with sides $a$, $b$, $c$
is right-angled.
</theorem>
</minipage>

*Long-form with the optional name kwarg. Cross-referenceable
via id (the "thm:" colon-prefix convention is consistent with
"fig:", "eqn:", "sec:" elsewhere in enscribe). The name
kwarg lifts to `data-name`; the Phase-2 handler will render
it as the "(Pythagoras)" suffix to the label "Theorem N".*

</section>
